**[Question 1] - What is the problem?**

Can a HARMONIC-style robot team learn a causal, uncertainty-aware interaction model that remains robust to multimodal distribution shifts and novel disturbances while producing faithful natural-language explanations of its own plans, actions, teammate beliefs, and failures?

**[Question 2] - Why is it interesting and important?**

HARMONIC correctly separates strategic cognitive reasoning from tactical control, but a trustworthy robot still needs a shared account of how observations, goals, beliefs, actions, and outcomes are related across that interface. Current vision-language-action systems can map language and images to actions, yet their fluent explanations are often post-hoc rationalizations, and their performance can collapse under changes in lighting, viewpoints, wording, sensor quality, or unexpected interventions. Explainable HRI studies (for example, work showing that explanations can change perceived human-likeness without establishing competence or safety) further suggest that trust cannot be evaluated by subjective ratings alone.

Solving this problem would connect cognitive architectures, robust multimodal learning, causal representation learning, explainable planning, and multi-robot collaboration. It would provide a testable definition of a faithful explanation: an explanation should identify the decision-relevant evidence and remain consistent with counterfactual changes to that evidence. The resulting system could support safer search-and-rescue, inspection, maintenance, and household assistance, where a human must understand why a robot acted, detect a mistaken belief, and redirect the team. A public benchmark and intervention-based metrics would let future work compare cognitive architectures and VLAs on reliability and accountability rather than task success or linguistic fluency alone.

**[Question 3] - Why is it hard?**

The problem couples several difficult forms of uncertainty. The robot must ground ambiguous language in partial, asynchronous observations; distinguish a teammate's goal, knowledge, and intention from its own beliefs; coordinate a drone and UGV with different action spaces and viewpoints; and convert high-level plans into safe low-level behavior. Novel disturbances may alter the causal structure itself, not merely add noise. A camera corruption, blocked route, misleading teammate report, or changed object appearance can therefore require belief revision and replanning.

Naively prompting an LLM or VLA to explain an action can produce plausible text that is unrelated to the computation that caused the action. Correlational attention maps do not establish that a stated object, belief, or goal was necessary, and ordinary imitation training rarely contains interventions that identify causal dependencies. A graph that is too rigid will fail under novel tasks; a graph learned only from observational trajectories will confuse correlation with cause. The system must also keep explanations synchronized with rapidly changing execution, quantify calibrated uncertainty, preserve safety when communication is delayed, and avoid exposing private or irrelevant teammate information. These requirements create a three-way tradeoff among real-time latency, expressive strategic reasoning, and verifiable faithfulness.

**[Question 4] - Why hasn't it been solved before?**

Prior cognitive architectures, including DIARC-like systems and the HARMONIC proposal, provide modular reasoning, memory, communication, and strategic/tactical control, but they do not establish a shared learned causal state with quantitative tests of explanation faithfulness under intervention. Classical explainable planners can expose symbolic preconditions and effects, but they generally assume a known, fully specified model and do not robustly ground open-ended language, pixels, uncertain teammate beliefs, or learned skills. Conversely, LLM/VLA robot systems offer broad language and visual competence but commonly use opaque policies, weakly specified memory, and post-hoc textual explanations. Human-robot studies often evaluate trust, likability, or acceptance without testing whether explanations track the actual decision process.

The missing combination is an end-to-end benchmark in which explanations, beliefs, and plans are tested by changing the alleged cause and observing whether the robot changes appropriately. Existing robustness work typically targets classification or single-agent perception, while existing multi-robot benchmarks emphasize completion and communication, not causal explanation under adversarial and out-of-distribution conditions. This proposal differs by making the strategic–tactical interface an auditable causal contract: every action carries provenance, uncertainty, and a counterfactual explanation, and both behavior and explanation are evaluated after controlled interventions.

**[Question 5] - What are the key components of my approach and results?**

We will build Causal-Robust HARMONIC (CR-HARMONIC), retaining independent strategic and tactical layers. Its strategic layer maintains a typed interaction graph with nodes for observations, entities, goals, subgoals, teammate beliefs, actions, outcomes, and explanations; edges encode temporal, evidential, delegation, and candidate causal relations. A belief-state estimator updates the graph from camera, proprioceptive, language, and inter-robot messages. An uncertainty-aware causal module learns invariant relations from trajectories containing randomized interventions, while a robust multimodal adapter is trained with visual corruptions, paraphrases, sensor dropout, misleading reports, and held-out domain styles. The planner selects graph-supported actions and attaches evidence, alternatives, confidence, and predicted outcomes. A counterfactual explainer edits one graph variable (for example, key location, route availability, or teammate belief), re-runs the planner or a lightweight causal simulator, and reports whether and why the action would change. The tactical layer checks feasibility and safety and returns execution outcomes and failure causes to the graph.

The primary benchmark will be a procedurally generated simulator modeled on the introduction's lost-keys scenario: a UGV and drone search, communicate, navigate, inspect, deliver, and recover objects across buildings and outdoor areas. Episodes will vary task composition, layouts, object appearances, natural-language instructions, agent capabilities, weather/lighting, communication delay, and teammate reliability. Training uses one set of layouts, object lexicons, disturbance families, and mission templates; validation holds out combinations; test includes unseen layouts and compositional missions, novel corruptions, adversarially misleading messages, blocked routes, sensor failures, and human interventions. We will complement this with ALFRED, TEACh, and Habitat/BEHAVIOR-style language-conditioned tasks where applicable, and, if hardware is available, a small UGV–drone or wheeled-robot–overhead-camera replication to test sim-to-real transfer.

We will compare CR-HARMONIC with (i) language-conditioned systems such as SayCan, PaLM-E, RT-2, Inner Monologue, and Open X-Embodiment-style policies where interfaces permit, (ii) an LLM/VLA planner with prompting, (iii) behavior cloning, (iv) a symbolic/task planner without learned multimodal grounding, (v) a HARMONIC/DIARC-style modular system without the causal graph, and (vi) robust-training-only, domain-adaptation-only, and perfect-perception oracle controls. Metrics will include mission success and weighted utility, collision and safety-constraint violations, attack/corruption success, worst-group performance, recovery after intervention, performance on compositional and novel-disturbance splits, completion time, path/energy cost, communication turns and bandwidth, clarification/defer precision and cost, strategic-to-tactical latency, and sim-to-real degradation. Calibration will use ECE, Brier score, NLL, selective risk/coverage, and robustness curves. Explanation quality will be measured by factuality against logged causal provenance, sufficiency and necessity under graph interventions, counterfactual consistency, modality/message deletion tests, localization of relevant evidence, human ability to predict the robot's next action and correct its belief, and calibrated confidence, alongside linguistic clarity and appropriate abstention. We expect the full model to preserve higher success and calibration under shifts, recover faster from interventions, reduce unnecessary communication, and produce explanations whose counterfactual faithfulness substantially exceeds fluent black-box and no-intervention baselines, with an explicit latency–faithfulness curve revealing the cost of verification.
